damaged_code:
```rust
struct SplineReticulator {
    is_gradient_saturated: bool,
}

impl SplineReticulator {
    pub fn saturate_gradient(&mut self) { self.is_gradient_saturated = true; }
    pub fn reticulate_splines(&self) {
        if !self.is_gradient_saturated {
            panic!("Must saturate gradient first!");
        }
    }
}

// Calling logic out of order is accepted by the compiler but fails at runtime
let mut worker = SplineReticulator { is_gradient_saturated: false };
worker.reticulate_splines(); // Panics: saturate_gradient() wasn't run first
worker.saturate_gradient();
```

counterexample:
```rust
// Enforcing sequential constraints by propagating states in type signatures
let builder = SplineBuilder::new();
let gradient = builder.saturate_gradient();
let worker = gradient.reticulate_splines(); // Type system prevents ordering errors
```
A consuming builder or typestate API in which a preparation method returns the only type exposing the next operation. Event loops or stream processes whose external event order is inherently unpredictable may legitimately validate transitions at runtime.
